sections.s2_technical:
Both reject duplicates using `hashCode()` and `equals()`. The difference shows only when you iterate.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.LinkedHashSet;
import java.util.Set;

Set<String> hs = new HashSet<>();
hs.add("SAV2001"); hs.add("CUR3001"); hs.add("FD4001");
// iteration order of hs is unpredictable

Set<String> lhs = new LinkedHashSet<>();
lhs.add("SAV2001"); lhs.add("CUR3001"); lhs.add("FD4001");
// iteration order of lhs is always SAV2001, CUR3001, FD4001
```

The senior point: choose LinkedHashSet whenever a deterministic, reproducible order matters for audit logs or statements, because unpredictable HashSet ordering makes output hard to test and review.

